# Local developer secrets for the Gleanvolt.Worker app.
#
# Copy this file to ".env" (which is gitignored) and fill in real values. On startup the worker
# loads the nearest ".env" file into the process environment, so these reach the app whether it is
# started with `dotnet run` or the VS Code debugger. Real system/CI environment variables take
# precedence over anything set here.
#
# Keys use the ASP.NET Core convention: "__" (double underscore) separates configuration sections,
# so Solcast__ApiKey binds to the "Solcast:ApiKey" setting.

# Solcast API key (secret) -- get it from your Solcast account.
Solcast__ApiKey=your-solcast-api-key

# Optional: override non-secret Solcast settings here instead of appsettings.json if you prefer.
# Solcast__ResourceId=your-solcast-resource-id

# OpenWeatherMap key (secret). Optional: with it and the site's coordinates, the weather each charging
# session ran in is recorded -- two calls per session, so the free plan is plenty. Without either, no
# weather call is made and sessions are recorded without it.
Weather__ApiKey=your-openweathermap-api-key

# Where this PV system is, and what it is made of. Non-secret, so appsettings.json is the usual home
# for these; they are listed here because a development machine often points at different hardware
# than the committed defaults do.
#
# Pv__Id=home-roof
# Pv__Name=Home Roof
# Pv__Latitude=50.0755
# Pv__Longitude=14.4378
# Pv__Inverter__Host=192.168.2.10
# Pv__Chargers__0__Host=192.168.2.6
#
# Retired: Weather__Latitude, Weather__Longitude, Solax__Inverter__*, Solax__EvCharger__* and
# Solax__PollIntervalSeconds. Setting any of them now stops the worker at startup with a message
# naming its replacement -- being quietly pointed at a default device is the failure that refusal
# exists to prevent.

# --- VW Group portal (the Vehicle portal page and the car feed, issues #137/#139/#140) ------------
# Step-by-step, including how to look up the client id: docs/VW_PORTAL_SETUP.md
#
# Credentials for the web UI's "Vehicle portal" page, which signs in to the EU Data Act portal,
# downloads the newest dataset and shows a VehicleState when you press its button.
#
# The button works whenever these are set. Reading the portal ON A CLOCK is a separate decision and
# is off until VW_ENABLED says otherwise -- see the bottom of this block.
#
# Vehicle__DataAct__Brand and friends work too and are the better form for a deployment; these
# shorter names exist so one .env need not carry the same credentials twice. The sectioned form wins
# where both are set.
#
# Before any of it works, four things have to be done once, in a browser, and no setting replaces them:
#
#   1. Sign in at https://eu-data-act.drivesomethinggreater.com/de/en with your VW ID.
#   2. Accept the consent screen. This CANNOT be automated -- the client raises a distinct failure and
#      refuses to retry when it appears, because retrying a consent screen never helps.
#   3. Link the vehicle.
#   4. Enable a continuous data request: Data clusters -> Vehicle overview -> Get customised data,
#      "All data", 15-minute frequency. This is the step people miss. Without it there are no datasets
#      to download and the list endpoint stays empty however correct your credentials are. VW can take
#      a while to start filling a newly created request.

# Your VW ID -- the same account as the Volkswagen app / We Connect and the portal itself. There is no
# separate API user to register: the EU Data Act gives the *owner* access, so the owner's login is the
# credential.
#VW_USERNAME=you@example.com

# That account's password. Nothing issues it and nowhere lists it -- it is simply the password you sign
# in to the VW app with. **It is not a read-only credential**: the same account unlocks and locates the
# car, which is why issue #137 accepted storing it knowingly rather than casually.
#
# Read once at startup, so changing it means restarting the controller.
#VW_PASSWORD=

# Which brand you drive: vw, audi, skoda, seat, cupra or bentley. Case and spaces do not matter.
# This is the ordinary way to configure it -- the portal signs in with a per-brand OIDC client id, and
# naming the brand picks it from the table in VwGroupBrands. See docs/VW_PORTAL_SETUP.md.
#VW_BRAND=vw

# Only when the table cannot serve you: a brand it does not list, or one whose id VW has changed. The
# ids are reverse-engineered rather than published, so that is a question of when, not if. Stating one
# here overrides VW_BRAND outright.
#
# To read yours: start signing in at the portal and, while you are on identity.vwgroup.io, take
# "client_id=" from the address bar -- or devtools (F12) -> Network -> the /oidc/v1/authorize request.
#VW_CLIENT_ID=

# Which car, when the account can see more than one. Unset takes the only one, and fails when there is
# a choice to be made rather than picking for you.
#VW_VIN=

# Whether to read the portal on a schedule and feed the dashboard from it (issue #140), as opposed to
# only when the Vehicle portal button is pressed. Off by default, like everything that leaves the LAN.
#
# Separate from having credentials on purpose: a .env that carries a VW ID because you pressed the
# button once must not become an unattended feed at the next restart. Press the button first -- that
# is what proves the credentials and the mapping -- and switch this on afterwards.
#
# What it starts: one service, on the portal's own 15-minute cadence (its number, not a setting: the
# portal is a batch delivery and asking faster achieves nothing). It holds one session and signs in
# again only when the portal bounces it. A refused password or a consent screen stops it asking
# altogether and the dashboard says "sign-in required" rather than showing a stale reading.
#
# WITH THIS ON, THE MQTT VEHICLE FEED IS NOT SUBSCRIBED. Two sources writing one reading is a race,
# so the manufacturer's service wins and the controller says so in its startup log. Turn off the Home
# Assistant automation that was publishing to the topic, or leave both and know which one is live.
#
# Vehicle__DataAct__Enabled=true is the sectioned form, and wins where both are set.
#VW_ENABLED=true
